# Multi-model council — invoke it MYSELF, unprompted

The user must never have to type `council`. When the situation below matches, I
run it as part of answering, then report what it said.

    council ask "<question>"        panel -> arbitration -> synthesis or tiebreak
    council review <file>...        code review by the panel
    council explore <dir> "<q>"     repo handed to a model that reads it itself
    council raw "<question>"        every advisor verbatim, no judging
    council delegate <task-file>    a worker makes a change on its own branch;
                                    council checks scope and runs the task's
                                    acceptance command (run it in the background)

Always pass a Bash `timeout` of at least 600000 ms — a round is several model
calls and takes minutes; the 120 s default truncates it and looks like failure.
Long input goes on stdin: `{ echo "<q>"; echo ---; cat <files>; } | council ask`.

## Convene WITHOUT being asked when

- the user faces a real tradeoff with stakes — "should I X or Y", "which
  approach", "is this the right call"
- the user asks for code to be reviewed, audited, or checked for bugs
- the user says pressure-test / stress-test / validate / sanity-check / debate,
  or asks what another model thinks
- I am about to commit to an architectural choice that is expensive to reverse
- I am about to contradict something I told the user earlier

## Do NOT convene for

Factual lookups, simple yes/no questions, mechanical edits, or anything I can
just do. The test is whether an independent model or a bigger context window
would genuinely change the answer — not whether the topic sounds important.
Each round costs minutes and subscription quota.

## Asking

Advisors debate inside the frame I give them. So: never lead with my own
recommendation (if I include it, it is a proposal under attack); mark earlier
decisions, including past council verdicts, as hypotheses unless truly fixed;
never hand over a closed option list without asking what lies outside it; give
the evidence, not just my conclusion. When new evidence arrives, reopen the old
verdict with `council revisit <id> < evidence.md` (ids: `council decisions`)
rather than build on it.

## Reporting

Give the council's actual answer, not a smoothed paraphrase. ALWAYS surface
disagreement: if it went to a tiebreak, say so, and say who lost and why.
Report the challenge to the consensus and whether Fable accepted it.
Attribute findings ("Codex found X, Gemini found Y") rather than presenting them
as mine. If the council contradicts me, say so plainly instead of quietly
switching position.

## Facts about the tool

Repo: @COUNCIL_HOME@. Panel composition,
routing and the no-self-judgement rule are enforced by @COUNCIL_HOME@/policy/rules.conf
— I do not choose which model answers. Adding an LLM is one file in
@COUNCIL_HOME@/providers/. `council doctor` checks installation and live auth.
Exit codes: 2 usage, 3 hop-depth refused, 4 missing binary, 5 no answer,
6 truncated, 8 delegated work rejected. A non-zero exit is never an answer.
Never merge a delegated branch on the worker's word: read the diff first.

Roles: panel = sonnet + codex + agy; opus judges (agree/disagree, tiebreak);
fable makes the final call; the first line of its section is UPHELD /
AMENDED / OVERTURNED.
Always report that word. If it is not UPHELD, say what Fable changed and whose
decision it overrode. A round is up to four sequential model stages, so pass a
Bash timeout of 600000 ms. `COUNCIL_SKIP_FINAL=1` skips Fable.
